# ADM-Cube research experiment fill: keep the console's running slots occupied.
# Install or refresh with: /home/lzp/miniconda3/envs/quant/bin/python ops/cron/install_cron.py --block research
#
# The round files below, in order, are the experiment queue. Each file's `--fill`
# asks /api/health how many of the console's slots and GPUs are free, skips
# the experiments whose directory already exists and creates the next
# pending ones in file order, through the same validation the operator gets
# from `--dry-run`; a local experiment the local-model limit holds, or a GPU experiment too
# few free cards hold, stays pending and the experiments behind it go ahead. The next
# file reads the slots afresh, so it takes what the earlier ones left. An experiment
# whose prebuilt view cache is still being built stays pending too, and is created
# by the first run after the prebuild reports status ok. Before an experiment is
# created its starter is replayed on its seed (`_round.smoke`, recorded under
# logs/research/smoke/), so the run that creates it can take tens of minutes;
# `flock -n` keeps the later runs out meanwhile. Nothing
# pending or nothing free is the steady state and exits 0; a refused creation
# in any file makes the run exit non-zero with its reason in the log, and the
# files after it still fill. The operator tops the queue up by appending experiments
# and their reference workspaces to a file, or a file to ROUNDS; a round whose experiments
# have all been created, or whose remaining experiments are withdrawn, is marked closed
# and leaves ROUNDS in the same change.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
CRON_TZ=Asia/Shanghai
REPO_ROOT=/Data2/lzp/auto-trade
QUANT_PYTHON=/home/lzp/miniconda3/envs/quant/bin/python
# The queue's round files, filled in this order; separated by spaces. Empty
# while nothing is queued: the job then exits 0 without creating anything.
ROUNDS=scripts/experiments/create_round_20261113.py

# Every ten minutes, all day: a slot that frees up stays empty for at most that
# long, and research experiments end at no particular hour. One run takes seconds per
# file unless it creates, and `flock -n` skips a run while an earlier one is
# still creating, so two fills can never take the same slot.
*/10 * * * * cd $REPO_ROOT && mkdir -p logs/research .runtime/research && flock -n .runtime/research/cron.lock bash -c 'status=0; for round in $ROUNDS; do $QUANT_PYTHON $round 38888 --fill || status=1; done; exit $status' >> logs/research/cron.log 2>&1
